now  is that most people tend to feel more  comfortable with conspiracy theories  that line up with where they are  politically and socially already so you  know conspiracy theories garner support  from people on the right if they are  right leaning the same with on the Left  what  I do see however is that most people  can't make money as conspiracy  entrepreneurs solely from just going on  YouTube are having a periscope they're  really successful ones are people like  Alex Jones who also sell a product Alex  Jones you know I think most people know  by now most of the money that he makes  is from sales of supplements and other  products that are sort of nominally  health oriented yeah there are I mean a  lot of conspiracy theories have nothing  to do with religion per se but it's  interesting that one of the ways that  some of these religious media outlets  are also monetizing is by using sort of  I don't know whether the conspiracy  theory is the right term but but sort of  apocalyptic fears and rapture sort of  imagery and that type of thing also to  be selling a product and it seems that  that is a sort of integral part of the  Entrepreneurship component yeah it's  very common and you'll see a lot of  overlap among those groups there is a  big sort of apocalyptic especially  Christian element and a lot of American  right-wing conspiracy culture but I mean  fundamentally one of the core aspects of  conspiracism is this idea that some  massive consequential damaging change is  right around the corner and that if  action is not taken right now something  terrible would have will happen which is  of course a really profitable way to  sell all kinds of things is by creating  the sense of urgency and fear and panic  can you give an example of I mean I  don't know no that I necessarily want to  brand all conspiracy theories as either  sort of a real world dangerous or real  world harmless it seems there's probably  some kind of spectrum but at least to  kind of get into that discussion can you  give an example of a prevalent  conspiracy theory that does not seem to  connect to sort of like real-world  danger either for the believers in it or  for others bystanders as we might call  them and then on the other side one that  does have significant real-world  consequences mmm like you say it's  really really hard to classify things  that way because a lot of people have  sort of overlapping conspiratorial  beliefs you know a lot of times people  will antal enter through one subculture  and end up elsewhere and it's really  hard to tell who is going to take real  damaging actions I mean for instance  Flat Earth is the subculture that is  very small but it is growing we know  there are more flat earthers than there  were a few years ago and on its surface  that one is pretty silly but it is  harmless more or less you know it's a  little bit alarming but it is harmless  however I and other people who cover  this have noted that there is a lot of  Cola cost denial in those worlds that  something about flat earther ism tends  to lead to a kind of question everything  mentality that goes into some really  weird and potentially dark places and of  course once you get into Holocaust  denial you are more susceptible to white  nationalist ideas right um so it can be  really hard to say but yeah on its face  Flat Earth is probably not a huge cause  for concern I would say things like  pizza gate and cue and I on the other  hand have been mostly harmless but  obviously there have been some pretty  sizable exceptions to that mainly Edgar  Welch who went to comet ping-pong in DC  the supposed sight of this Democrat run  child sex slave ring with a gun you know  intent on saving children and so I would  say that the the subcultures that make  me the most nervous are obviously the  ones that are tight with tied with white  nationalism and extremism as well as the  ones that brand their enemies as child  abusers because when you are talking  about child abuse when you were accusing  someone of being a pedophile or someone  who hurts children um it justifies any  kind of action against them there is  almost nothing that isn't justified to  stop that kind of behavior and so it  sets up this very apocalyptic  potentially violent rhetoric that does  make me a little bit nervous here's what  I don't understand about the sort of  transfer from one conspiracy theory to  belief in another so you mentioned the  idea of you get in through Flat Earth  and then all of a sudden you end up with  like Holocaust denial why do we not seem  to see and if it exists I would love to  hear about it you don't see people who  for example enter via Flat Earth and  then come to the realization that huge  corporations that been  that from cheap immigrant labor love the  idea that there is this sort of argument  or debate between low and middle income  people and immigrants over who is  actually responsible for keeping wages  low and stealing jobs that's a real  thing where we have these huge  corporations benefitting by sort of  fomenting that battle right you don't  really see the traditional conspiracy  theorists ever end up with that one  which happens to be true they end up  with something more cartoonish like  Holocaust denial why is that the path  and not the one that I mentioned I mean  it could be that some individual  conspiracy consumers do come to more or  less the place that you're talking about  you know people who for instance entered  through something like 9/11 truther ISM  from the documentary loose change you  know which was pretty influential for a  bunch of people in that world not all of  those people became hardcore 9/11  truthers some of them came to believe  that there was yeah as you say just a  political and social reason why those  why those conspiracy theories spread I  think really I would say that we don't  see people sort of loudly and publicly  leaving conspiracy communities for a  more reality based version of the world  because it the algorithms that spread  conspiracy theories don't really reward  those kinds of claims or proclamations  somebody who is a really really big  conspiracy youtuber just sort of doesn't  have any particular motivation to come  out and say that they were wrong and  they've actually come to a different and  more grounded view of the world can you  quantify in any way the effect that the  Trump president presidency has had on  belief in conspiracy theories and  conspiratorial thinking so we know that  conspiracy theories wax and wane in  America depending on sort of times of  social upheaval times of social and  people's social unrest when we're  questioning who we are as people we tend  to see a rise in conspiracism and  typically whatever political party is  out of power is more prone to conspiracy  theorizing so we see a little bit more  of it on the left and we did during the  Obama years what is really unique about  the term presidency of course is that he  himself uses conspiracy theories  spread them and seems to see them as a  way to solidify his base solidify  support for him and to try to identify a  common enemy to encourage his supporters  to be mad at you know instead of him a  scapegoat which is something that we see  a lot in more authoritarian countries  it's unusual but not unheard of for a  u.s. president and it really sort of  raises the stakes of what conspiracy  theories mean you know if Trump wakes up  tomorrow and decides to start tweeting  that vaccines cause autism or you know  that Q really not really done by the way  which he's previously done yes he  previously did that before he was  president and has not talked about it  too much since he's become president  which is a very good thing but yeah if  he wakes up and decides to start  promoting a more virulent conspiracy  theory from the Oval Office that will  have huge effects obviously you  mentioned that it's it's unusual but not  without precedent to see a president  pushing stuff like this what are other  examples of presidents I mean Nixon is  obviously the most obvious example of a  conspiracy theorist president he was  someone who believed you know that there  was a communist plot against him that  was actually orchestrated by Jews um  when Andrew Jackson someone tried to  assassinate Andrew Jackson and he came  to believe that it was functionally the  deep state  you know functionally that there was  some kind of plot within government to  assassinate him instead of a lone gunman  so we see it like here and there  throughout American history um something  that is really sort of unusual about  Trump is that we can see it in real time  and we can see it via social media how  he's attempting to sort of use these  theories for his own gain yeah that's  been a definite change in terms of the  environment around the president for  sure absolutely fascinating stuff we've  been speaking with an emergen who is a  senior reporter at Geo Media Group also  author of the new book Republic of Lies  which I highly encourage you to check  out Anna thank you so much for talking  to me today thank you for having me I  appreciate it

